First, I want Anaconda to the python version that installed on my machine.
That's a good starting point. You want Anaconda to use the Python version already installed on your Linux Mint machine, rather than using a different Python version bundled with Anaconda.

There is one important distinction to understand first:

System Python — installed and managed by Linux Mint/Ubuntu, used by system utilities and OS components.

Conda Python — installed and managed by Anaconda, normally isolated from the system Python.

Anaconda does not normally switch its base environment to use your system Python directly. Conda manages its own Python interpreter. However, you can create a Conda environment using a Python version that matches your system's Python version, which is usually the safest approach.

Step 1 — Check the Python version installed on Linux Mint
Open your Linux Mint Terminal and run:

python3 --version

Then run:

which python3

This will tell us the system Python version and where it is installed.

Step 2 — Check Anaconda's Python version
Now run the following commands:

conda --version

conda info

And finally:

python --version

The last command should be run inside your Anaconda Prompt/Terminal after Anaconda has been activated.

